\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\f0\b0\fs28\fi-1560\li2380\fc0\cf0 SUMMARY	Enqueue a DSP command\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1960 DECLARED IN	sound/snddriver_client.h\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1940\li2760 SYNOPSIS	kern_return_t 
\b snddriver_dsp_host_cmd(
\b0 port_t 
\i commandPort
\i0 , u_int 
\i hostCommand
\i0 , u_int€
\i priority
\b\i0 )\

\fs30 \

\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380 DESCRIPTION	
\b snddriver_dsp_host_cmd()
\b0  enqueues a command on the sound driver's DSP command queue that interrupts the DSP and causes it to execute one of 32 interrupt routines (or 
\i host commands
\i0 ).  Its arguments are as follows:\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\fi-380\li2760\fc0\cf0 ·	
\i commandPort
\i0  is the DSP command port, as retrieved by 
\b snddriver_get_dsp_cmd_port()
\b0 .  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	
\i hostCommand
\i0  is an integer that represents the host command you want to execute.  The first 22 host commands are already defined (or reserved).  The host commands provided by NeXT are represented by constants (prefix ªDSP_hc_º) that are defined in 
\b /usr/include/nextdev/snd_dsp.h
\b0 .  Creating your own host command requires a familiarity with DSP programming that lies beyond the scope of this description.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	
\i priority
\i0  is one of the three priority constants SNDDRIVER_LOW_PRIORITY, SNDDRIVER_MED_PRIORITY, or SNDDRIVER_HIGH_PRIORITY.  The sound driver sorts the commands in its DSP command queue according to priority.\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi0\li2380 When the DSP receives a host command, it sets the HC flag in the Command Vector Register.  After executing the command, the DSP clears the flag.  You should always precede a call to 
\b snddriver_dsp_host_cmd()
\b0  with a call to 
\b snddriver_dspcmd_req_condition()
\b0  that waits for HC to clear in order to avoid overwriting a previously requested, but as yet unexecuted, host command:\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx3140\tx3900\tx4400\tx4900\tx5400\tx5920\tx6420\tx6920\tx7420\tx7920\tx8440\tx8940\tx9440\tx9940\tx10440\tx10960\f4\fs24\li2760\fc0\cf0 /* CVR_HC is defined in <nextdev/snd_dspreg.h> */ \
err = snddriver_dspcmd_req_condition(commandPort, CVR_HC, 0, ...);\
if (err != 0)\
    . . .\
\
/* Now enqueue the host command request. */\
err = snddriver_dsp_host_cmd(...);\
if (err != 0)\
    . . .\

\fs30 \

\pard\tx2380\f0\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	Returns an error code:  0 on success, nonzero on failure.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\b\fs28\fi-1540 SEE ALSO	snddriver_dspcmd_req_condition()\

\fs20 \

\fs60 \

\pard\f2\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 \

{{\NeXTHelpMarker5510 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_function:snddriver_dsp_protocol();}
¬}\f2\b\i0\ulnone\ql\fs28\fi0\li2380\gray0\fc0\cf0\up0\dn0 snddriver_dsp_protocol()\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\f0\b0\fs28\fi-1560\li2380\fc0\cf0 SUMMARY	Set the sound driver's protocol vis-a-vis the DSP\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1960 DECLARED IN	sound/snddriver_client.h\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1940\li2760 SYNOPSIS	kern_return_t 
\b snddriver_dsp_protocol(
\b0 port_t 
\i devicePort
\i0 , port_t 
\i ownerPort
\i0 , int€
\i protocol
\b\i0 )\

\fs30 \

\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380 DESCRIPTION	
\b snddriver_dsp_protocol()
\b0  lets you establish the manner in which the sound driver communicates with the DSP; specifically, it determines whether to create 0, 1, or 2 DSP-reply buffers and whether DSP interrupts are enabled.  The existence of the DSP-reply buffers determines whether you can use streams to transfer data.  \

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 The function's first two arguments are the sound driver device port and the DSP owner port, as acquired through 
\b SNDAcquire()
\b0 .  \

\fs20 \

\fs28  
\i protocol
\i0  is the heart of the matter:  It's a code that represents the protocol that you wish to establish.  There are two ways to create the appropriate protocol:  If you're using streams to access the DSP, then you should pass the protocol variable that's modified by calls to 
\b snddriver_stream_setup()
\b0 , as explained (with an example) in the description of that function.  AlternativelyÐor in addition to the foregoingÐyou can create a protocol code by or'ing the following DSP protocol constants:\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi-380\li2760 ·	SNDDRIVER_DSP_PROTO_RAW represents the barest protocol.  The sound driver makes no assumptions about how the DSP is being used:  No DSP-reply buffers are created and the DSP can't interrupt the host.  You can't use streams in raw protocol; to transfer data, you use the 
\b snddriver_dsp_write()
\b0  and 
\b snddriver_dsp_read()
\b0  functions.\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi0\li2380 All the other protocols create at least one DSP-reply buffer and allow DSP interrupts, thus allowing you to transfer data through a stream:\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi-380\li2760 ·	SNDDRIVER_DSP_PROTO_DSPMSG (ªDSP-messageº) creates a buffer that can hold 512 DSP-reply messages.  A message from the DSP (as it lies in the reply buffer) is a 24-bit word right-justified in 32 bits.  To receive the contents of this buffer, you enqueue a request through 
\b snddriver_dspcmd_req_msg()
\b0 .\

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	SNDDRIVER_DSP_PROTO_DSPERR (ªDSP-errorº) creates an additional 512-message DSP-reply buffer that collects error messages sent from the DSP.  An error message is identified as having its MSB (bit 23) set.  You can request the contents of the error buffer through 
\b snddriver_dspcmd_req_err()
\b0 .\

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	SNDDRIVER_DSP_PROTO_C_DMA (ªcomplex DMAº) implies DSP message mode (a single DSP-reply buffer is created) and allows DSP-initiated DMA transfers.  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	SNDDRIVER_DSP_PROTO_HFABORT (ªhost flag abortº) causes the driver to take note if the DSP aborts.  (The DSP indicates that it has aborted by setting HF2 and HF3.)\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi0\li2380 To get the documented behavior from these protocols, you 
\i must
\i0  include SNDDRIVER_DSP_PROTO_RAW.  \

\fs20 \

\pard\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 Note:  A protocol of 0 produces Release 1.0 behavior; this is roughly equivalent to a combination of DSP message, DSP error, and host flag abort modes.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	Returns an error code:  0 on success, nonzero on failure.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\b\fs28\fi-1540 SEE ALSO	snddriver_stream_setup()\

\fs20 \

