\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\f0\b0\fs28\fi-1560\li2380\fc0\cf0 SUMMARY	Set the DSP host flags\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1960 DECLARED IN	sound/snddriver_client.h\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1940\li2760 SYNOPSIS	kern_return_t 
\b snddriver_dsp_set_flags(
\b0 port_t 
\i commandPort
\i0 , u_int 
\i flagMask
\i0 , u_int€
\i flagValue
\i0 , u_int 
\i priority
\b\i0 )\

\fs30 \

\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380 DESCRIPTION	
\b snddriver_dsp_set_flags()
\b0  enqueues a command to modify one or both of the DSP host interface flags HF0 (host flag 0) and HF1 (host flag 1).\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 The 
\i flagMask
\i0  argument defines which of the host flags you want to affect.  The flags are represented by the constants SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF0 and SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF1.  You can set both flags at the same time by or'ing these two constants.  (ICR stands for ªInterrupt Control Registerº; this is the register to which the host flags belong.)\

\fs20 \

\i\fs28 flagValue
\i0  is the value to which you're setting the flag(s).  A host flag can be either on or off, states that are also referred to as ªsetº and ªclearedº.  To set a flag, you pass its constant identifier; to clear it, you pass 0.  The following examples illustrate this concept:\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx3140\tx3900\tx4400\tx4900\tx5400\tx5920\tx6420\tx6920\tx7420\tx7920\tx8440\tx8940\tx9440\tx9940\tx10440\tx10960\f4\fs24\li2760\fc0\cf0 /* Set HF0 (turn it on). */\
snddriver_dsp_set_flags(..., SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF0,\
           SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF0,...)\
\
/* Clear HF1. */\
snddriver_dsp_set_flags(..., SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF1,0,...)\
\
/* Set both flags. */\
snddriver_dsp_set_flags(..., \
        SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF0 | SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF1,\
           SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF0 | SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF1, ...)\
\
/* Set HF0 and clear HF1. */\
snddriver_dsp_set_flags(..., \
        SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF0 | SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF1,\
           SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF0, ...)\
\
/* Clear both flags. */\
snddriver_dsp_set_flags(..., \
        SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF0 | SNDDRIVER_ICR_HF1, 0,...)\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\f0\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 The other two arguments, 
\i commandPort
\i0  and 
\i priority
\i0 , are the DSP command port and command-queue priority, respectively.  The DSP command port is retrieved through 
\b snddriver_dsp_cmd_port()
\b0 ; you set the priority to one of SNDDRIVER_HIGH_PRIORITY, SNDDRIVER_MED_PRIORITY, or SNDDRIVER_LOW_PRIORITY.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	Returns an error code:  0 on success, nonzero on failure.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\b\fs28\fi-1540 SEE ALSO	snddriver_dspcmd_req_condition()\

\fs20 \

