\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\f0\b0\fs28\fi-1560\li2380\fc0\cf0 SUMMARY	Request the contents of the DSP-reply buffers \

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1960 DECLARED IN	sound/snddriver_client.h\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1940\li2760 SYNOPSIS	kern_return_t 
\b snddriver_dspcmd_req_msg(
\b0 port_t 
\i commandPort
\i0 , port_t 
\i replyPort
\b\i0 )\

\pard\b0\fi-380\li2760\fc0\cf0 kern_return_t 
\b snddriver_dspcmd_req_err(
\b0 port_t 
\i commandPort
\i0 , port_t 
\i replyPort
\b\i0 )\

\fs30 \

\pard\tx2380\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380\fc0\cf0 DESCRIPTION	The 
\b snddriver_dspcmd_req_msg()
\b0  and 
\b snddriver_dspcmd_req_err()
\b0  functions are part of the mechanism by which your application retrieves messages from the sound driver's DSP-reply buffers.  They request that the contents of the appropriate buffer (as described below) be sent in a Mach message to 
\i replyPort
\i0 , a valid port that must already be allocated.  Simply requesting a message is only half of the story:  You then have to receive the message that's been sent, usually by sitting in a 
\b msg_receive()
\b0  loop.  You typically process the Mach messages that these functions induce by passing the messages to the 
\b snddriver_reply_handler()
\b0  function.\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 The utility of these functions depends on your application's DSP protocol:  \

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi-380\li2760 ·	You should never use these functions in raw protocol since the sound driver doesn't create any DSP-reply buffers.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	By including DSP message protocol, a single DSP-reply buffer is created in which both€error and non-error messages are stored; thus 
\b ...req_msg()
\b0  is of use, but 
\b ...req_err()
\b0  isn't.  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	DSP error protocol deems that two buffers be created, one for error messages and the other for non-error messages.  Both functions are useful in this protocol.  \

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi0\li2380 DSP protocol and how to set it is explained in the description of the 
\b snddriver_set_dsp_protocol()
\b0  function.  For both functions, the 
\i commandPort
\i0  argument is the DSP command port as retrieved by 
\b snddriver_get_dsp_cmd_port()
\b0 .  \

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	Returns an error code:  0 on success, nonzero on failure.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\b\fs28\fi-1540 SEE ALSO	snddriver_set_dsp_protocol(), snddriver_reply_handler() \

\fs20 \

\fs60 \

{\pard\f2\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0{\NeXTHelpMarker18971 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_function:snddriver_get_dsp_cmd_port();}
¬}\f2\b\i0\ulnone\ql\fs28\fi-760\li3140\gray0\fc0\cf0\up0\dn0 snddriver_get_dsp_cmd_port()\

