\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\f0\b0\fs28\fi-1560\li2380\fc0\cf0 SUMMARY	Get the DSP command port \

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1960 DECLARED IN	sound/snddriver_client.h\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1940\li2760 SYNOPSIS	kern_return_t 
\b snddriver_get_dsp_cmd_port(
\b0 port_t 
\i devicePort
\i0 , port_t 
\i ownerPort
\i0 , port_t€*
\i commandPort
\b\i0 )\

\fs30 \

\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380 DESCRIPTION	
\b snddriver_get_dsp_cmd_port()
\b0  attempts to get the 
\i DSP command port
\i0 , the port through which the sound driver issues commands to the DSP.  If it's successful, the port is returned in the 
\i commandPort
\i0  argument, which needn't have been previously allocated.  \

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 The first two arguments, 
\i devicePort
\i0  and 
\i ownerPort
\i0 , are the sound driver device port and the DSP owner port, as acquired through 
\b SNDAcquire()
\b0 .  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 The DSP command port is required as an argument by almost all sound driver functions that communicate with the DSP.  The one notable exception, for which you don't have to get the command port as it's gotten implicitly when needed, is if you send and retrieve DSP data via streams after having booted the DSP through the 
\b SNDBootDSP()
\b0  sound library function.  But even in this case getting the command port as a reflex to getting the DSP owner port won't serve you ill.  \

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	Returns an error code:  0 on success, nonzero on failure.\

\fs20 \

\fs60 \

{\pard\f2\b\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0{\NeXTHelpMarker20137 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_function:snddriver_new_device_port();}
¬}\f2\b\i0\ulnone\ql\fs28\fi-760\li3140\gray0\fc0\cf0\up0\dn0 snddriver_new_device_port()\

