\pard\tx2380\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380\fc0\cf0 DESCRIPTION	
\b snddriver_stream_control()
\b0  provides control over an active stream by allowing you to apply a controlling operation to one or more of the stream's enqueued regions.  The stream and the regions therein are identified by the function's first two arguments:  \

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\fi-380\li2760\fc0\cf0 ·	
\i streamPort
\i0  is the stream's port, as created by 
\b snddriver_stream_setup()
\b0 .\

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	
\i regionTag
\i0  is the integer identifier that you gave the region (or regions) in a previous call to 
\b snddriver_stream_start_writing()
\b0  or 
\b snddriver_stream_start_reading()
\b0 .  \

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi0\li2380 A tag value of 0 causes the controlling operation to be applied to all regions enqueued on the stream.  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 You specify the controlling operation by passing one of the following constants as the control argument:\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi-380\li2760 ·	SNDDRIVER_PAUSE_STREAM causes the stream to pause.  If data is currently being read from or written to the specified region, the read or write is immediately suspended.  If the region isn't yet active, the pause takes effect when the region comes to the top of the stream's queue (it's paused just before the first sample is read or written).  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	SNDDRIVER_RESUME_STREAM resumes a previously paused stream.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	SNDDRIVER_ABORT_STREAM terminates the stream's activity when the specified region comes to the top of the queue; the queue is then cleared.  If the region is currently being acted upon, the stream is terminated immediately.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	SNDDRIVER_AWAIT_STREAM is used to retrieve a partially recorded region from a stream that's reading dataÐnormally, you can't retrieve such data until the entire region has been filled.  If the specified region is currently active, a data-recorded message is sent to the reply port that you registered in 
\b snddriver_stream_start_reading()
\b0 .  You then pass the message to 
\b snddriver_reply_handler()
\b0  which calls the 
\b recorded_data
\b0  reply handler.  The unrecorded portion of the region continues.  If the specified region isn't currently active, this has no effect.\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi0\li2380 While you can use any of these four at the same time by or'ing them in 
\i control
\i0 , the only combination that's of use is SNDDRIVER_AWAIT_STREAM or'd with one of the other three.  For example, by setting 
\i regionTag
\i0  to 0 and 
\i control
\i0  to\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx3140\tx3900\tx4400\tx4900\tx5400\tx5920\tx6420\tx6920\tx7420\tx7920\tx8440\tx8940\tx9440\tx9940\tx10440\tx10960\f4\fs24\li2760\fc0\cf0 SNDDRIVER_PAUSE_STREAM | SNDDRIVER_AWAIT_STREAM\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\f0\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 you immediately pause the stream and can then bring back data from the current region.  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 You can request that a stream-paused, stream-resumed, or stream-aborted message be sent to the reply port when you pause, resume, or abort a stream, respectively, by setting the appropriate 
\i msg...
\i0  flag to true in your call to 
\b snddriver_stream_start_...()
\b0 .  \

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 snddriver_stream_nsamples()
\b0  returns the number of bytes (
\i not
\i0  samples, despite the name€of the function) that have been read from or written to a particular stream.  The steam€is specified by 
\i streamPort
\i0 .  The byte count is returned by reference in the 
\i byteCount
\i0 €argument.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	Returns an error code:  0 on success, nonzero on failure.\

\fs20 \

